The '''International Federation of Competitive Bodybuilding''' ('''IFCB''') is the international governing body for professional and amateur bodybuilding. Founded on June 14, 1958, by [[Harlan Cooke]] and representatives from twelve national bodybuilding federations, the IFCB is headquartered in [[Portsmith]], [[United Midlands]]. It sanctions the [[Mr. Adonia]] competition — the most prestigious title in professional bodybuilding — as well as its female equivalent, [[Ms. Adonia]], and a global circuit of qualifying events.

As of 2025, the IFCB comprises 142 national federations across six continental confederations. It is the sole bodybuilding federation recognized by the Adonian International Sports Council (AISC) for the sanctioning of international bodybuilding competitions.

==History==

===Founding (1958–1964)===

The IFCB was established on June 14, 1958, at a meeting in [[Portsmith]], [[United Midlands]], convened by [[Harlan Cooke]], a former competitive bodybuilder and gym owner. Cooke had organized the amateur Adonian Physique Championship since 1951 and sought to create a formal international structure for the sport, which had previously been governed by a loose network of regional promoters.

The founding meeting was attended by representatives from twelve national federations: the [[United Midlands]], [[Breisland]], [[Varkana]], [[Echia]], [[Andalus]], [[Nakamizu]], [[Okaiken]], [[Balisca]], [[Burawa]], [[Dagit]], [[Yanbaru]], and [[Kaneda]]. The delegates approved a constitution establishing the IFCB as the sport's international governing body, with Cooke elected as its first president.

The IFCB's first major initiative was the creation of the [[Mr. Adonia]] competition in 1965, designed as a professional successor to the amateur Adonian Physique Championship. The inaugural contest was held on September 12, 1965, at the Portsmith Grand Hall.

===Expansion (1965–1980)===

The 1965 launch of Mr. Adonia brought immediate international attention to the IFCB. Membership grew from 12 to 38 national federations by 1970, and the federation established its first continental confederations: the Adonian Bodybuilding Confederation (ABC) in 1966, the Baliscan Bodybuilding Union (BBU) in 1968, and the East Adonian Bodybuilding Federation (EABF) in 1970.

In 1974, the IFCB introduced the dual weight division system for Mr. Adonia, splitting competitors into Heavyweight and Lightweight categories. The system remained in place until 1979, when it was replaced by a single open division.

The 1975 Mr. Adonia, held in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]], was the first IFCB-sanctioned event staged outside the [[United Midlands]] and was documented in the sports film ''Iron and Will'' (1977). The event's international success prompted the IFCB to expand its qualifying circuit beyond its traditional base.

===Professionalization (1980–1995)===

The 1980s saw the IFCB transition from a volunteer-run organization to a professional sports federation. The IFCB Professional League was established in 1982 as a subsidiary body responsible for professional competitions, athlete contracts, and prize money distribution. [[Harlan Cooke]] stepped down as president in 1983, succeeded by [[Henrik Vossberg]] of [[Breisland]], the sculptor who had designed the Adonis Statuette.

The federation's growth during this period was driven by the dominance of [[Marcus Crane]] (1984–1991) and [[Rick Dixon]] (1992–1997), whose rivalries and personalities brought bodybuilding to mainstream audiences. Television broadcasting of Mr. Adonia began in 1985, and the IFCB negotiated its first international broadcast deal in 1988.

In 1991, the IFCB adopted formal anti-doping regulations, becoming one of the first international sports federations to implement mandatory drug testing for all professional competitors. The regulations were introduced in response to growing concerns about performance-enhancing substances in the "Mass Monster" era.

===Global era (1995–present)===

The IFCB's membership surpassed 100 national federations in 1998, coinciding with the rise of [[Titus Grol]] and the sport's expanding international profile. The federation launched its official website in 1999 and began live-streaming qualifying events in 2003.

The 2007 victory of [[Kazuki Hino]] at Mr. Adonia triggered a surge in bodybuilding participation across [[Okaiken]] and East Adonia. The IFCB responded by establishing the East Adonian Bodybuilding Academy in [[Okami]] in 2009, the federation's first regional training and development centre.

In 2012, the IFCB introduced the 212 Division for competitors weighing under 212 lb, followed by the Men's Physique division in 2013 and the Classic Physique division in 2016. These additions were part of a broader strategy to diversify the sport's competitive categories and attract a wider athlete base.

The 2024 Mr. Adonia, broadcast to an estimated 40 million viewers worldwide, marked the IFCB's largest commercial event to date. The federation's annual revenue was reported at $48 million in 2024, with prize money across all divisions totalling $4.2 million.

==Structure==

===Governance===

The IFCB is governed by a General Assembly, comprising one representative from each member federation, which meets biennially. The General Assembly elects the Executive Board, consisting of a president, three vice presidents, a secretary general, and six continental representatives. The Executive Board is responsible for the federation's day-to-day operations and meets quarterly.

The current president is [[Henrik Vossberg Jr.]], son of the former president and statuette sculptor, elected in 2022. The vice presidents represent the federation's three largest continental confederations: the Adonian Bodybuilding Confederation, the Baliscan Bodybuilding Union, and the East Adonian Bodybuilding Federation.

The IFCB's headquarters in [[Portsmith]] houses the federation's administrative offices, the Professional League's operations centre, and the IFCB Museum of Bodybuilding, which opened in 2010.

===Divisions===

The IFCB operates through two primary divisions:

* '''IFCB Professional League''' — Established in 1982, the Professional League oversees all professional bodybuilding competitions, including Mr. Adonia, Ms. Adonia, and the global qualifying circuit. It manages athlete contracts, prize money distribution, and professional rankings. The Professional League is governed by a board of directors appointed by the Executive Board.

* '''IFCB Amateur Division''' — The Amateur Division oversees amateur bodybuilding competitions, including the Adonian Physique Championship (the successor to the original amateur competition), national championships, and developmental programmes. It is responsible for athlete development, coaching certification, and grassroots promotion of the sport.

