===Adonian Physique Championship===

The Adonian Physique Championship is the IFCB's premier amateur bodybuilding competition, held annually since 1951 (predating the IFCB's founding). It serves as the primary pathway for amateur bodybuilders to earn professional status. The overall winner of the Adonian Physique Championship receives an IFCB Professional League card, granting eligibility to compete in professional events, including Mr. Adonia.

The championship is held in a different host country each year, with recent editions hosted in [[Concepción]], [[Balisca]] (2024), [[Capua]], [[Echia]] (2023), and [[Yanbaru]], [[Okaiken]] (2022).

===Regional championships===

Each continental confederation organizes its own regional championship:

* '''ABC Championship''' — Adonian Bodybuilding Confederation, held biennially since 1967
* '''BBU Championship''' — Baliscan Bodybuilding Union, held biennially since 1969
* '''EABF Championship''' — East Adonian Bodybuilding Federation, held biennially since 1971
* '''CABA Championship''' — Central Adonian Bodybuilding Association, held biennially since 1975
* '''VCBF Championship''' — Varkan-Charxian Bodybuilding Federation, held biennially since 1979
* '''BDBC Championship''' — Burawa-Dagit Bodybuilding Confederation, held biennially since 1983

==Anti-doping==

The IFCB implemented its first formal anti-doping policy in 1991, becoming one of the earliest international sports federations to adopt mandatory drug testing for all professional competitors. The policy was introduced in response to the "Mass Monster" era of the late 1980s and early 1990s, during which the use of performance-enhancing substances was widely believed to be endemic in the sport.

The IFCB's anti-doping programme is administered by the IFCB Anti-Doping Commission, an independent body reporting to the Executive Board. The commission conducts random and targeted testing at all IFCB-sanctioned events, as well as out-of-competition testing throughout the year. Athletes who test positive for banned substances face suspensions ranging from two years to lifetime bans, depending on the severity and frequency of violations.

Despite the IFCB's efforts, bodybuilding remains a sport where the use of performance-enhancing substances is a persistent concern. The federation has acknowledged the challenges of enforcement and has called for greater international cooperation in anti-doping efforts.

